SOURCE="Philippine Statistics Authority";
NOTEX="<strong>Indicator:</strong> Per capita GDP##<strong>Definition:</strong>##Per capita GDP is a measure that indicates the average economic output generated per person within the province/HUC.##See more: <a href='https://psa.gov.ph/system/files/eiad/Quarte"
"rly-and-Annual-Gross-Domestic-Product-Sources-and-Methods.pdf'>https://psa.gov.ph/system/files/eiad/Quarterly-and-Annual-Gross-Domestic-Product-Sources-and-Methods.pdf</a>##<strong>Method of Computation:</strong>##Per capita GDP is derived by dividing th"
"e GDP of a province/HUC by its total population.##<strong>Source of Basic Data:</strong>##Data for GDP are sourced from both survey and administrative sources. Major sources for the benchmark estimates include Census of Philippine Business Industry (CPBI"
"), Updated list of establishments (ULE), Family Income and Expenditure Survey (FIES), while surveys and administrative data from various stakeholders were utilized in non-benchmark compilation.#Population data is based on the results of the population ce"
"nsus.##<strong>Note/s:</strong>##i. Provincial Product Accounting (PPA) is an accounting framework, which is aligned with the Philippine System of National Accounts (PSNA), that aims to present a comprehensive picture of the provincial and Highly Urbaniz"
"ed City (HUC) economies.#ii. The population data used for the computation of GDP per capita are based on the preliminary 2020-based CPH population projection.#iii. Figures for the City of Makati exclude the Embo Barangays starting in 2022, following thei"
"r transfer to the City of Taguig.";
